WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.429 --> 00:00:04.950
The Middle East is one of the most complex
regions in the world:

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:10.350
Currently there are 4 failing states and 3
wars, with major powers increasingly taking

00:00:10.350 --> 00:00:12.000
opposite sides.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.080
Countless armed militias and terrorist groups
are spreading violence across borders.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.490
The region has seen conflict after conflict
going back well into the 20th century.

00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:24.640
But among all the uprisings, civil wars, and
insurgencies, two countries always seem to

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.610
be involved: Saudi Arabia and Iran.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:31.720
They’re bitter rivals, and their feud is
the key to understanding conflicts in the

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:33.000
Middle East.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.210
The Saudis and Iranians have never actually
declared war on each other.

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:41.149
Instead, they fight indirectly by supporting
opposing sides in other countries and inciting

00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:42.210
conflicts.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.480
This is known as proxy warfare.

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.260
And it’s had a devastating effect on the
region.

00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:50.480
Countries, especially poor ones, can’t function if there are larger countries pulling strings

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:51.520
within their borders.

00:00:51.640 --> 00:00:54.460
Both the Saudis and the Iranians,

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.000
see these civil wars as both tremendous threats,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.700
and also potentially enormous opportunities.

00:01:01.719 --> 00:01:05.920
The Saudi-Iranian rivalry has become a fight over influence, and the whole region is a

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:06.920
battlefield.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:09.540
It’s why the rivalry is being called: a
Cold War.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:18.160
The most famous cold war was fought for 40

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:20.880
years between the United States and Soviet
Union.

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:25.180
Looking forward to the day when their flag would fly over the entire world.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.351
They never declared war on each other, but

00:01:27.351 --> 00:01:29.740
clashed in proxy wars around the world.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.710
Each side supported dictators, rebel groups, and intervened in civil wars to contain the

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.740
other.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:40.500
Like the US and Soviet Union, Saudi Arabia
and Iran are two powerful rivals - but instead

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.200
of fighting for world dominance, they’re
fighting over control of the Middle East.

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:49.420
In order to understand the Saudi-Iranian rivalry,
let’s go back to the origins of each country.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:54.470
In the early 1900s, the Arabian peninsula
was a patchwork of tribes under the control

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.450
of the Ottoman Empire.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:02:00.990
After World War I, the empire collapsed, leaving these tribes to fight each other for power.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.430
One tribe from the interior, the al-Saud,
eventually conquered most of the peninsula.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:08.569
In 1932, they were recognized as the Kingdom
of Saudi Arabia.

00:02:08.569 --> 00:02:13.730
6 years later, massive oil reserves were discovered in Saudi Arabia, and, in an instant, the Saudi

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.059
monarchy was rich.

00:02:15.059 --> 00:02:19.260
That oil money built roads and cities 
all around the desert country - and

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.019
it helped forge an alliance with the US.

00:02:21.019 --> 00:02:25.109
On the eastern side of the Persian Gulf, another country was emerging, but having a much harder

00:02:25.109 --> 00:02:26.109
time.

00:02:26.109 --> 00:02:30.019
Iran also had massive oil reserves and an
even bigger Muslim population.

00:02:30.019 --> 00:02:32.319
But constant foreign intervention was creating chaos.

00:02:32.319 --> 00:02:37.189
Since the 18th century, Iran had been invaded
by the Russians and British twice.

00:02:37.189 --> 00:02:43.809
In 1953, the US secretly staged a coup, removing the popular prime minister, Mohammed Mosaddegh.

00:02:43.809 --> 00:02:48.040
In his place, they propped up a monarch, Reza Shah, who was aggressively reforming Iran

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:50.400
into a secular, westernized country.

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:54.560
But he harbored corruption and terrorized
the population with his secret police, the

00:02:54.560 --> 00:02:55.560
Savak.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.079
By the 1970s, both Saudi Arabia and Iran had oil-based economies and had governments heavily

00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:04.239
backed by the US, but the feelings among each population were very different:

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:09.339
Ultimately at the end of the day, the
Shah of Iran, powerful as he was, simply

00:03:09.339 --> 00:03:16.180
did not have the same control over his people
or ultimately the same legitimacy and affection

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:20.359
that the Saudi people felt towards their monarchy
at that point in time.

00:03:20.359 --> 00:03:24.669
That’s because Iran’s Muslims felt stifled
by the Shah’s reformations and by the end

00:03:24.669 --> 00:03:27.520
of the decade, they finally fought back.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.040
Iran's Islamic revolution overthrew a powerful regime,

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.480
that boasted military might.

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:37.100
It’s really in 1979, when Ayatollah Khomeini and the Islamic revolution

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:43.099
overthrow the Shah, that the real tension
between Saudi Arabia and Iran begins.

00:03:43.099 --> 00:03:47.599
Ayatollah Khomeini was a Muslim clergyman,
who preached against Western-backed secular

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:49.120
monarchies.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.280
He advocated for a government that popular, Islamic, and led by the clergy.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:57.480
And In 1979, he led a revolution to establish just that.

00:03:57.489 --> 00:04:02.029
It was a massive international event that
prompted reactions around the world especially

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:03.029
in Saudi Arabia.

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:07.500
The Iranian Revolution terrified the government
of Saudi Arabia.

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:10.540
They were fearful that Ayatollah Khomeini

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:14.200
would inspire their populations to rise up against them,

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:17.600
exactly the way he had caused the Iranian population

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.560
to rise up against the Shah.

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:22.210
There was a religious threat too.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:26.130
Up until now, the Saudis had claimed to be
the leaders of the Muslim world.

00:04:26.130 --> 00:04:29.560
Largely because Islam’s two holiest sites,
Mecca and Medina are in Saudi Arabia.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:33.729
But Khomeini claimed his popular revolution
made Iran the legitimate Muslim state.

00:04:33.729 --> 00:04:38.410
There was another divide; Saudi Arabia’s
population is mostly Sunni, the majority sect

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.080
of Islam, while Khomeini and Iran are mostly
Shia.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:46.260
Westerners always make a
mistake by drawing an analogy between the

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.580
Sunni-Shia split and the Protestant-Catholic
split within Christianity.

00:04:51.580 --> 00:04:54.860
The Sunni-Shia split was never as violent.

00:04:54.940 --> 00:04:56.860
And in much of the Islamic world,

00:04:56.860 --> 00:05:00.300
when Sunnis and Shia were living in close proximity,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:03.160
they got along famously well.

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:07.940
So, while the Sunni-Shia split was not a reason
for the rivalry, it was an important division.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:12.620
After the revolution, the Saudi’s fears
came to life when Iran began “exporting

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:13.629
its revolution”.

00:05:13.629 --> 00:05:19.219
This CIA report from 1980 details how the
Iranian started helping groups, mostly Shia,

00:05:19.220 --> 00:05:22.340
trying to overthrow governments in Iraq, Afghanistan, and Saudi Arabia.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.020
And they prompted the Saudis

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:27.960
to redouble their efforts, to fight against Iran.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.540
They bolstered their alliance with the US
and formed the GCC, an alliance with other

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.219
gulf monarchies.

00:05:34.220 --> 00:05:35.980
The stage was set for conflict.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:39.900
War in the gulf. Iraq invaded Iran in seven areas.

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:43.300
With a 5:1 superiority, Iraqi forces moved quickly

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.780
The rise of Iran as a regional power threatened

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.550
other neighboring countries as well.

00:05:47.550 --> 00:05:53.370
In September 1980, Iraq, under the rule of
dictator Saddam Hussein, invaded Iran.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:57.640
He was hoping to stop the Iranian revolution,
gain power, and annex some of Iran’s oil

00:05:57.640 --> 00:05:58.640
reserves.

00:05:58.640 --> 00:05:59.780
But they didn’t get far.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.170
The war bogged down into stalemate complete
with trench warfare, chemical weapons and

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:07.249
heavy civilian casualties.

00:06:07.249 --> 00:06:11.430
When Iran started winning, the Saudis panicked,
and came to Iraq’s rescue.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:13.910
They provided money, weapons, and logistical
help.

00:06:13.910 --> 00:06:19.280
So it becomes critical to the Saudis that
they build up Iraq, and build it up into a

00:06:19.280 --> 00:06:25.580
wall that can hold back the Iranian torrent that
they have unleashed.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.560
The Saudi help allowed Iraq to fight until
1988.

00:06:28.560 --> 00:06:31.900
By then, nearly a million people had died.

00:06:31.900 --> 00:06:35.090
Iranians largely blamed the Saudis for the
war and the feud escalated.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.270
Fast forward 15 years and Iraq again became
the scene of a proxy war.

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:43.230
In 2003 the US invaded Iraq and overthrew

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:44.909
Saddam Hussein.

00:06:44.909 --> 00:06:49.150
Neither Saudi Arabia or Iran wanted this to
happen, since Iraq had been acting as a buffer

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:50.379
between them.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:53.120
But problems arose when the US struggled to
replace Saddam.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:57.780
The United States has no idea what it is doing in Iraq after 2003.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:02.420
And it makes one mistake after another, that creates a security vacuum,

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:07.580
and a failed state, and drives Iraq into all-out civil war.

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:11.669
Without a government, armed militias took
control of Iraq, splintering the population.

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:14.969
Sunni and Shia militias suddenly sprang up
all over the country.

00:07:14.969 --> 00:07:19.849
Many were radical Islamist groups who saw
an opportunity to gain power amidst the chaos.

00:07:19.849 --> 00:07:23.800
These militias were readymade proxies for
Saudi Arabia and Iran, and they both seized

00:07:23.800 --> 00:07:26.430
the opportunity to try and gain power.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:31.379
The Saudis started sending money and weapons
to the Sunni militias, and Iran; the Shia.

00:07:31.379 --> 00:07:35.870
Iraq was suddenly a proxy war with Saudi Arabia
and Iran supporting opposing sides.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.629
That trend continued into the Arab Spring,
a series of anti-monarchy, pro-democracy protests

00:07:40.629 --> 00:07:43.160
that swept through the Middle East in 2011.

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:46.199
This had very different consequences for Saudi
Arabia and Iran:

00:07:46.199 --> 00:07:49.810
That is terrifying to the Saudis who are the

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:52.009
ultimate status quo power.

00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:56.289
They want the region stable, and they don't want anbody

00:07:56.289 --> 00:08:00.620
rising up and overthrowing a sclerotic, autocratic government,

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.699
for fear that it might inspire their own people

00:08:03.700 --> 00:08:04.700
to do the same.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:08.580
The Iranians are the ultimate anti-status quo power,

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.479
they have been trying for decades to overturn

00:08:11.479 --> 00:08:13.150
the regional order.

00:08:13.150 --> 00:08:16.849
Each country threw their weight behind different
groups, all over the Middle East.

00:08:16.849 --> 00:08:21.090
Just like in Iraq, the Saudis began supporting
Sunni groups and governments while Iran helps

00:08:21.090 --> 00:08:23.080
Shia groups rise up against them.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:27.780
In Tunisia, the Saudi’s backed a dictator
while the Iranians stoked protests.

00:08:27.780 --> 00:08:32.430
In Bahrain, Iran supported Shia leaders seeking
to overthrow the government.

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:35.820
Saudi Arabia, in turn, sent troops to help
quash the unrest.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:39.250
Both got involved in Libya, Lebanon and Morocco

00:08:39.250 --> 00:08:42.979
As Saudi Arabia and Iran put more and more
pressure on these countries… they began

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:43.980
to collapse.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:49.060
Now the feud has gone a step further, with
both countries deploying their own militaries.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:52.450
In Yemen, the Saudi military is on the ground
helping the central government.

00:08:52.450 --> 00:08:56.339
They are fighting the rebels, called the Houthis,
who are an Iranian proxy group.

00:08:56.339 --> 00:09:01.710
The reverse is happening in Syria. The Iranian
military is fighting side by side with militias,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:06.940
some of them extremists groups like Hezbollah,
in support of dictator Bashar al-Assad.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:10.630
They are fighting rebel Sunni groups, who
are Saudi proxies.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:13.990
The more civil wars that broke out in the
Middle East, the more Saudi Arabia and Iran

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:14.980
became involved.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:18.700
Neither the government of Saudi Arabia nor the government

00:09:18.709 --> 00:09:20.710
of Iran are looking for a fight.

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:27.709
But the problem is these civil wars create
circumstances that no one could have predicted.

00:09:27.709 --> 00:09:32.860
Both the Iranians and the Saudis feel that their vital national interests,

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:35.970
are threatened, are in jeopardy,

00:09:35.970 --> 00:09:38.260
because of different things happening in these civil
wars,

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:41.950
things they blame each other for.

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.639
Now the cold war is drawing in other countries.

00:09:44.639 --> 00:09:48.510
The Saudi government is threatening Qatar, a tiny Gulf state that was developing ties

00:09:48.510 --> 00:09:49.510
with Iran.

00:09:49.510 --> 00:09:53.930
Meanwhile in Syria and Iraq, the terrorist
group, ISIS is nearing defeat and both the

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.640
Saudis and Iranians are angling to take control
of that territory.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:10:00.220
It’s a Cold war that's becoming incredibly
unpredictable.

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.640
As the Middle East continues to destabilize,
its hard to say how far these countries will go.

